 amendment to HB 1002 that would have blocked further exemptions. The caucus press release quotes him: \"Apparently, it’s fine for trillion-dollar corporations to pay no sales tax on energy use at their data centers.\" This puts him against rung 5 (\"Welcoming data center investment with minimal regulatory barriers\"). It matches none of the clauses that separate rungs 1–4: a moratorium, a ban on passing costs to households, conditions before approval, or faster permits with disclosure.\n- **HB 1333:** his objection is that the bill weakens local control and skips public hearings. That says which level of government should decide, not what the rule should be, so I coded it `adjacent` under the annex's local-control hard case.\n- **HB 1002 vote:** this is a utility-affordability bill, not a data-center measure. His Yea vote is reported only on the caucus site, so it isn't a record either.\n- **`needs_source`:** two items:\n  - the House Journal roll call and amendment text for his data-center tax-exemption amendment to HB 1002;\n  - the HB 1333 roll call and bill text.\n- **Quotes:**\n  - The tax-exemption quote and the local-control quote are tier `direction` and quotable.\n  - The newsletter's description of the amendment (\"It also would have prevented…\") is tier `none` and not quotable, coded `not-forward`.\n\nSeparately, six claude.ai connectors (Benevity, Candid, Canva, Figma, Harvey, WorkOS) need to be authorized in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. The GitHub plugin failed to connect because of a malformed authorization header. None of these were needed for this task.","ttft_ms":13096,"type":"result","duration_ms":36043,"uuid":"8963b69c-9ac1-4089-8e4e-5286436aefdd","ttft_stream_ms":1416,"time_to_request_ms":77,"first_content_frame_ms":1416,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
